topics.explanation:
Eddy currents (Foucault currents) are loops of induced current that circulate inside the bulk of a conductor when the flux through it changes. By Lenz's law they oppose the motion or the change that causes them, producing damping and heating. Their useful applications include:

- electromagnetic damping in moving-coil galvanometers, which makes the pointer dead-beat;
- magnetic braking in electric trains;
- induction furnaces, which melt metals by heating;
- electric power meters.

Their harmful effect is energy lost as heat in transformer cores and motor armatures. This loss is reduced by using laminated cores, or cores with slots, which increase the resistance of the eddy-current paths. A magnet dropped through a vertical copper tube falls with an acceleration less than $g$ and can reach a terminal speed. Similarly, a metal plate swinging between magnet poles is damped quickly, while a slotted plate swings much more freely.

A transformer changes alternating voltage using mutual induction between primary and secondary coils wound on a common soft-iron core. For an ideal transformer, $\frac{V_s}{V_p} = \frac{N_s}{N_p} = \frac{I_p}{I_s}$, so $V_pI_p = V_sI_s$. A step-up transformer has $N_s > N_p$ and increases voltage while decreasing current. A step-down transformer does the reverse. Power and frequency are not changed. Transformers do not work on steady DC, because a constant flux induces no emf. Real transformers have four main losses, each with its own remedy:

- flux leakage, reduced by winding the coils one over the other;
- copper (joule) loss, reduced by using thick wires;
- eddy-current loss, reduced by a laminated core;
- hysteresis loss, reduced by a soft-iron core with a narrow hysteresis loop.

Efficiency is $\eta = \frac{P_{out}}{P_{in}}$. Power is transmitted over long distances at high voltage and low current to reduce $I^2R$ line losses.
